## Claim
Text: "The US saw the best carbon emission numbers in 35 years under President Donald Trump's administration."
Claim author: Donald Trump
Claim date: October 23, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### Were the absolute carbon dioxide emissions in the United States during any year of Donald Trump's presidency (2017–2020) lower than the annual emissions recorded in any year between 1985 and 2016?
Answer: According to the provided search result, "US CO2 emissions in 2019 were the lowest they have been since 1992." This indicates that while emissions during Trump's presidency (specifically in 2019) were lower than those in the years from 1993 to 2016, they were not lower than the annual emissions recorded in the years from 1985 to 1991.

Source URL: https://www.climatechangenews.com/2020/10/06/us-emissions-four-years-president-trump/

### How do the per capita carbon dioxide emissions in the United States during the Donald Trump administration compare to the per capita emissions recorded annually between 1985 and 2016?
Answer: During the Donald Trump administration, specifically in 2019, the United States' per capita carbon dioxide emissions were lower than any annual per capita emissions recorded between 1985 and 2016. According to the search result, "[p]er capita emissions were lower in 2019 than they’ve been at any time since at least 1950."

Source URL: https://www.climatechangenews.com/2020/10/06/us-emissions-four-years-president-trump/

### How did the rate of decline in United States carbon dioxide emissions during the Donald Trump administration compare to the rate of decline during the Barack Obama administration?
Answer: The rate of decline in United States carbon dioxide emissions was slower during the Donald Trump administration than during the Barack Obama administration. According to the provided source, "US emissions have declined more slowly during Trump’s term than when Barack Obama was in the White House," with CO2 emissions falling by 11% over Obama's eight years in office compared to a decline of "just 0.5%" during Trump's first three years in power.

Source URL: https://www.climatechangenews.com/2020/10/06/us-emissions-four-years-president-trump/

### What role did the ongoing market transition from coal-fired power generation to natural gas and renewable energy sources play in reducing United States carbon emissions between 2017 and 2020?
Answer: The ongoing market transition from coal to natural gas and renewable energy sources was the primary driver behind the reduction of U.S. greenhouse gas emissions during this period, particularly in 2019 when emissions from electric power generation dropped by 10%. According to the provided source, this decline was largely due to "the switch from coal to natural gas and renewables in the electric power sector," with natural gas obtained through fracking playing the dominant role in replacing "dirtier and more CO2-heavy coal," while "renewable energy is a much smaller factor" in the drop.

Source URL: https://www.aei.org/op-eds/us-emissions-are-falling-under-trump-thanks-to-frackings-war-on-coal/

