### Summary of Key Insights
The retrieved evidence shows that in 2019, US absolute carbon dioxide emissions reached their lowest levels since 1992 (27 years), while per capita emissions were the lowest since at least 1950 (70 years). However, this decline was not the result of the Trump administration's policies. Instead, it was primarily driven by market forces, specifically the transition from coal to natural gas and renewable energy, alongside state and local climate initiatives. In contrast, the Trump administration actively rolled back federal climate regulations, such as weakening vehicle fuel economy standards. Furthermore, the rate of emissions decline during Trump's first three years (0.5%) was substantially slower than the 11% decline achieved during the eight years of the Obama administration.

### Lacking Information
The evidence provided is clear, comprehensive, and sufficient to evaluate the claim. No further information is required.

### Conclusion
Based on the fact-check, the claim is decided to be `cherry-picking`. While the claim is technically true if referring specifically to per capita emissions (which were at their lowest since 1950), presenting this as a success of the Trump administration is highly misleading. It excludes the essential context that the reduction was driven by market forces rather than administration policies, that the Trump administration actively weakened emissions standards, and that the rate of decline actually slowed down significantly compared to the Obama administration.

### Verdict: CHERRY_PICKING

### Justification
While U.S. per capita carbon dioxide emissions in 2019 were indeed the lowest since at least 1950, absolute emissions were only at their lowest since 1992 (27 years), according to [Climate Change News](https://www.climatechangenews.com/2020/10/06/us-emissions-four-years-president-trump/). Furthermore, this decline was not driven by the Trump administration's policies, which actually sought to roll back environmental protections and weaken vehicle fuel economy standards, as reported by [NPR](https://www.npr.org/2020/03/31/824431240/trump-administration-weakens-auto-emissions-rolling-back-key-climate-policy). Instead, the reduction was primarily driven by market forces transitioning from coal to natural gas and renewables, as detailed by the [American Enterprise Institute](https://www.aei.org/op-eds/us-emissions-are-falling-under-trump-thanks-to-frackings-war-on-coal/). Additionally, the rate of emissions decline under the Trump administration (0.5% over three years) was significantly slower than the 11% decline seen during the Obama administration, meaning the claim cherry-picks per capita data while misleadingly attributing a market-driven decline to the administration's actions.